[Music]  look at those words  one year of coving let's think about  that for a second the now  year-long pandemic has taken a major  toll on the mental health  of the medical workers too a new review  of 65 studies from around the world  finds that  one in five health care workers have  experienced depression  anxiety ptsd or a combination of all of  those conditions  the new streaming program 60 minutes  plus shows us one doctor's struggle in  georgia  wesley lowry has her story aaron rayburn  rojas  a critical care doctor at northeast  georgia medical center  says her workload is still more than  double what it was pre-pandemic  with no relief in sight have there been  moments that  have felt like you're reaching a  breaking point or have been difficult or  where you've been overwhelmed  every day every day yeah it's not  uncommon for us to take a minute to go  cry  i mean i cry in my office all the time  everything you do is about getting  people better  and a lot of times it just doesn't  happen  the lack of being able to help someone  in their most vulnerable moments  is the injury the fact that it happens  over and over and over again  is what i think really causes the the  damage  and you've said that you think about  walking away from it sometimes yeah  how seriously do you consider that  depends on the day  as i'm preparing for my shift the night  before  i start to get a physical reaction to  even thinking about going to work  i start to get palpitations  and then i start to think about why am i  doing this i don't have to do this  there's been days where i've had to take  a personal day  just because the thought of coming back  in and doing the same thing again was  just too much  i don't think it substitutes you know  what will be needed  as far as debriefing and therapy after  this  you definitely plan to go to therapy oh  absolutely yeah  wow even the helpers need help 60  minutes plus correspondent wesley lowry  joins us now with more on the story  wesley it's good to see you  you know we just heard that one doctor  say i've often thought about walking  away i know you talked to many health  care workers  was that a common thing with them they  just think  about leaving of course you know  we i'm actually still down in northeast  georgia  where we've been talking to health care  workers in rural hospitals and we also  our team spoke with healthcare workers  across the country and we heard  time and time again you know we've asked  a just a monumental task of our medical  workers this last year  the amount of death they've seen the  pain they've seen the surges  and virtually every health care worker  we talked to said there are times where  they have thought about walking away  because it's just so difficult  but they don't so who is helping them  how are they getting through it  well and that's one of the big questions  and it's one of the big tasks and  challenges ahead of us as we  emerge from this pandemic is how do we  make sure to take care  of these health care workers many of  whom are traumatized  you know like many professions medical  work does have a stigma around it  as it relates to mental health folks who  are scared  to come forward and say i need help and  so there are  some organizations national associations  who are  asking for a kind of sweeping  attempt to chronicle uh the mental  health of health care workers asking for  money to be devoted there was some money  in the covid relief bill  but asking the biden administration  essentially to create a tracking program  like we did for the 911 first responders  making sure that  everyone who was on the front lines of  the pandemic we know where they are we  know what they're going through and we  can provide help for them  in our last piece wesley we had a health  official say we don't have a health care  system  we have a sick care system and what  you're learning or what you're hearing  is that  now it's actually threatening the  stability of what we still call  the health care system how so  well we have to remember that even  before the pandemic we were facing a  relative crisis in nursing  uh that we didn't have an overflow of  health care workers and now  we've asked them to do this herculean  job to respond to a pandemic  and a virus that we didn't really know  how to deal with initially and to do so  without resources without time without  ppe  and so we've taken what was already a  depleted workforce  and then handed them this unthinkable  task  and it's going to be unsurprising if in  the months to come  we see any number of healthcare workers  walking away further depleting the  system  making it harder for us to get care we  got to go wesley but i do want to know  what the workers told  you about the vaccine  they they're met all the workers we  talked to her most of them have been  vaccinated  they really really want folks to get  vaccinated  because what they worry about is the  summer holidays the spring holidays  happen they're worried that if people  aren't vaccinated they're gonna have  another search  right you don't have to tell me twice  thank you very much wesley good to see  you  if you or someone you know is seeking  mental health resources  you can call the national alliance on  mental illness the helpline at  1-800-950-6264  or in a crisis you can text nami that's  n-a-m-i  to 741-741 and wesley's full report is  on 60 minutes plus this sunday on the  new viacom cbs streaming service  paramount plus

